WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.679
Log data may include untrusted input, and that input should be handled as carefully as any other boundary value.

2
00:00:06.679 --> 00:00:16.170
A string that is safe for display in one place may not be safe as a log field, a line-oriented record, or a downstream search value.

3
00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:22.497
Newlines, tabs, terminal control characters, unusual encodings, and very long values can make log records confusing or misleading.

4
00:00:22.497 --> 00:00:28.824
Defensive logging encodes or escapes control characters and stores values in a form that preserves the record structure.

5
00:00:28.824 --> 00:00:30.582
Bound external values before logging.

6
00:00:30.582 --> 00:00:35.152
A username, file name, header value, or parser error can be unexpectedly large.

7
00:00:35.152 --> 00:00:41.830
Truncation should be explicit and visible, such as recording that a value was shortened rather than silently losing context.

8
00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.642
Canonicalization matters when logs are used for comparison.

9
00:00:44.642 --> 00:00:53.430
If the program normalizes case, trims whitespace, or resolves a path-like value before making a decision, logs should make clear whether the raw or

10
00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.188
canonical form is being recorded.

11
00:00:55.188 --> 00:00:57.297
Separate raw input from trusted fields.

12
00:00:57.297 --> 00:01:02.570
A field named user_message should not be confused with an event name or trusted status.

13
00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:09.248
Stable labels should come from code, while external data should be bounded, encoded, and stored under clearly named fields.

14
00:01:09.248 --> 00:01:13.467
The defensive principle is to treat log output as a data format.

15
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:15.927
It has structure, consumers, and integrity expectations.

16
00:01:15.927 --> 00:01:20.497
Preserving that structure helps maintainers trust what they see during troubleshooting and review.

17
00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:25.418
Integrity also depends on consistent encoding between the application, log library, collector, and viewer.

18
00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:32.800
If each layer handles special characters differently, an event that looked safe when written may become confusing when searched or exported.
